            <ArticleTitle>Intraabdominal focal fat infarction in a 75-year-old woman presenting as acute abdomen.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2096.e3-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ajem.2012.01.012</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0735-6757(12)00035-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Epiploic appendagitis is a rare, self-limiting inflammation of the epiploic appendices or omental appendices. It presents as abdominal pain often misdiagnosed as appendicitis, cholecystitis, or diverticulitis. Epiploic appendagitis can be treated conservatively with anti-inflammatory and pain medications. It is important to diagnose this etiology of abdominal pain in order to avoid long-term hospital stay and other medical expenses including surgery. In this case report we present a rare case of epiploic appendagitis that presents in a 75 year old female patient.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
